[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Azure Cosmos DB Consistency Models and Partitioning"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building globally distributed applications requires balancing data availability, latency, consistency, and scalability. Azure Cosmos DB solves these challenges by offering tunable consistency models and an automated, horizontal partitioning engine. This tutorial explores how these two pillars work together to deliver enterprise-grade performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Azure Cosmos DB Consistency Models"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike traditional databases that force a binary choice between strong and eventual consistency, Azure Cosmos DB provides five well-defined consistency models along the consistency spectrum. Developers can tune consistency at the request level to optimize for latency or data correctness."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strong: Linearizable reads. The read is guaranteed to return the most recent committed version of an item."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Bounded Staleness: Reads lag behind writes by a maximum of K operations or T time interval."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Session: Monotonic reads, monotonic writes, and read-your-writes guarantees for a specific client session."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consistent Prefix: Updates returned contain some prefix of all updates, with no gaps, maintaining causal order."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Eventual: Out-of-order reads with no ordering guarantees. Replicas eventually converge."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Default Consistency via Azure CLI"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can configure the default consistency level for your Azure Cosmos DB account using the Azure CLI. Here is how to set the default consistency to Bounded Staleness with a max staleness of 100,000 requests and a max interval of 10 seconds."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"az cosmosdb create --name "mycosmosdbaccount" --resource-group "myResourceGroup" --default-consistency-level "BoundedStaleness" --max-staleness-prefix 100000 --max-interval 10"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Mastering Partition Keys and Horizontal Scaling"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Azure Cosmos DB uses horizontal partitioning to scale throughput and storage infinitely. Every item in a container requires a partition key. The partition key determines which logical partition the item resides in, and multiple logical partitions map to physical partitions managed automatically by Azure."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Logical Partition: Consists of all items sharing the same partition key value. Limited to 20 GB of storage."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Physical Partition: Internal underlying resource managed by Azure. Each physical partition supports a maximum of 50,000 RU/s and 50 GB of storage."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Partitioned Container using .NET"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"When creating a container, choosing the correct partition key is vital. A good partition key has a high cardinality and distributes workload evenly across all partitions. Below is an example of creating a container with a partition key of "/departmentId" using the Azure Cosmos DB .NET SDK."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"CosmosClient client = new CosmosClient("your-connection-string");
Database database = await client.CreateDatabaseIfNotExistsAsync("companyDB");
ContainerProperties containerProperties = new ContainerProperties("employees", "/departmentId");

Container container = await database.CreateContainerIfNotExistsAsync(
    containerProperties,
    throughput: 400
);
Console.WriteLine("Container created with partition key /departmentId");"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Partitioning and Consistency"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Hotspots: Ensure your partition key receives a high distribution of requests to prevent single physical partition throttling."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage Session Consistency: Use Session consistency as your default for web and mobile apps to provide the best balance between performance and user experience."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Include Partition Key in Queries: Always include the partition key in your point reads and queries to avoid costly cross-partition queries."}]
